 ## Overview
-SWAG is an extension of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) designed to handle unstructured, in-the-wild image collections by modeling appearance variations and handling transient objects (occluders) without supervision [S1]. It sets itself apart from standard 3DGS by disentangling static scene geometry from dynamic appearance and transient occluders, using image-dependent embeddings and opacity modulation to prevent the "blurry elongated Gaussians" and floaters typical of view-dependent 3DGS in unconstrained settings [S1]. It builds on [3D Gaussian Splatting Implementation Details](9128-3d-gaussian-splatting-implementation-details.md) by adding a conditioning mechanism for color and opacity.
+SWAG is an extension of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) designed to reconstruct 3D scenes from unstructured, in-the-wild image collections [S1]. It addresses two primary limitations of standard 3DGS in unconstrained scenarios: photometric variations (changes in lighting and appearance across different captures) and the presence of transient occluders (e.g., tourists, clouds) [S1]. Unlike standard 3DGS, which averages appearance and creates floaters or blurry Gaussians to compensate for view-dependent effects, SWAG disentangles static scene geometry from dynamic appearance and transient objects using per-image conditioning [S1]. It sets itself apart from siblings like [3iGS](13977-3igs-factorised-tensorial-illumination-for-3d-gaussian-splat.md) by using a latent image embedding and an MLP to modulate color and opacity, rather than explicit illumination grids [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-SWAG modifies the 3DGS representation by introducing per-image conditioning for color and opacity, allowing the model to adapt to lighting changes and exclude transient objects like tourists or clouds [S1].
+SWAG modifies the standard 3DGS parameter set by introducing appearance-dependent color modulation and adaptive opacity modulation. The method optimizes a Hash Grid encoding, per-image embeddings, and an MLP alongside the standard Gaussian attributes [S1].
 
 ### Inputs and Outputs
-*   **Inputs:** A set of unstructured images $I_i$ with known camera poses, and an initial sparse point cloud from Structure-from-Motion (SfM) [S1].
-*   **Outputs:** A set of 3D Gaussians representing the static scene, and a mechanism to identify and optionally remove transient Gaussians [S1].
-
-### Data Structures
